{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And HaShem went</b>, etc. Its saying <b>to guide them</b> means — to provide them with restfulness of the way, for since the days were hot, HaShem hovered over them in a pillar of cloud so that the sun would not strike them. And therefore the pillar of cloud functioned only by day and not by night, and this cloud is distinct from the cloud that went before them to straighten the crooked places, to lower the high ground and raise the low ground, and to show them the way. And there is proof for this from what the verse says in the portion of Devarim (Deuteronomy 1:33): ״who went before you in the way, to search out for you a place to encamp, in fire by night, to show you the way you should go, and in cloud by day.״ Now the verse mentioned three: the first — to search out for you a place — this functioned constantly before them, meaning it went in advance ahead of them. The second — the pillar of fire to show you, etc. The third — and in cloud by day. And these are exactly our words, that this cloud was only to hover, shielding them from the sun. And not as Rashi explained, that לַנְחֹתָם is deficient a ה״א and he brought a parallel from לַרְאֹתְכֶם, for according to our words both here and there it is not deficient a ה״א. And see the verse that follows this one." "<b>And by night, etc., to illuminate, etc.</b> It is difficult according to their words, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 22b), who said that for all forty years that they were in the wilderness they did not journey except by His light, for they could see even what was inside the flask. And one cannot say that He is speaking about the nighttime, for this is not the plain sense of their saying ״they did not journey except by His light״ — for what does it come to exclude, if not the light of the sun by day? And if so, at night why did it not illuminate like day, so that they would not need the sun, that they required a pillar of fire to illuminate for them? And to understand the matter, there is further a fine point to examine in its saying <b>to journey by day and by night</b>: why was the explanation ״to illuminate for them״ not sufficient, that it needed to say ״to journey,״ etc.?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of the words is always that the light of HaShem, blessed be He, never departed from the camp of Israel, as our Sages, may their memory be for blessing, said, which we cited above. However, one must know that this holy light did not spread outside the camp of Israel but only within their settlements. And there is a reason for this, all the more so in the evil wilderness, which is the abode of the camp of impurity, for there the klippah came to rest, as is known from the words of the holy ones (Zohar vol. 1, 126a). Since this is so, Israel could not benefit from the supernal light except within their camp; but outside their camp, night would be dark according to the order of the world. Now if Israel did not journey by night whenever HaShem wished to travel, they would not have needed the light of the pillar of fire, for all the children of Israel had the light of HaShem in their settlements. Rather, because of their traveling by night, they needed the pillar to illuminate far off, outside the camp of Israel, to recognize the place where they would walk outside their camp. And this is its saying <b>to illuminate for them</b>, and the reason for this light is <b>to journey by day and by night</b> — meaning equally: just as by day, in addition to the light of HaShem that sparkled upon them, they could see far off by the light of the sun that illuminates the entire world, so too at night they could see far off through the medium of the pillar of fire. And with this one may explain what the verse says in the portion of Devarim (Deuteronomy 1:33): ״in fire by night to show you the way you should go,״ meaning לַרְאֹתְכֶם — in order that you may see, in order that you may see it — not as Rashi explained, לְהַרְאוֹתְכֶם, deficient a ה״א, bringing parallels from the Prophets and the Writings, for we have no need of this. For the verse explained its own words, since our interpretation is indicated by its saying ״to see [לַרְאֹתְכֶם] in the way״ — whereas according to Rashi's reading it should have said ״the way [הַדֶּרֶךְ]״. Rather, the intention is that Israel had eternal light in their settlements but not on the way where they had not yet arrived; therefore it says that the pillar of [fire] — its purpose was so that they might see in the way which they would walk upon, in the future tense. And understand, for these are exactly our own words. From that point on there was no difference for the children of Israel between day and night, and they could not distinguish the time of day and the time of night except by means of the changing of the clouds."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The Hebrew text reads עַמּוּד (עָנָן) [אֵשׁ] — i.e., it has 'pillar of cloud' with 'fire' given as the corrected reading in brackets. I translated according to the bracketed correction, consistent with the argument about nighttime illumination."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:52.442791163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}